WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:04.956
A bolted joint, cut open: a bolt and nut through two steel plates,

2
00:00:05.096 --> 00:00:06.803
with washers that spread the pressure.

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:08.666
Tighten the nut,

4
00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:12.869
and the bolt stretches a fraction of a millimetre: like a stiff spring,

5
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.750
it squeezes the plates together.

6
00:00:15.835 --> 00:00:17.902
That clamping force is the preload.

7
00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:21.263
It holds the joint together, not the torque.

8
00:00:22.041 --> 00:00:26.267
When it is high enough, nothing slips and the nut does not work loose.

9
00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:34.090
M10 by 1.5 is a thread 10 millimetres across, with a pitch of 1.5 millimetres.

10
00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:37.459
That is how far the nut moves in one turn.

11
00:00:38.468 --> 00:00:43.321
The head shows the property class, to ISO 898, part 1.

12
00:00:44.038 --> 00:00:48.775
The first number times a hundred is the tensile strength: for eight point eight,

13
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:50.643
800 megapascals.

14
00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:53.031
That is where the bolt breaks.

15
00:00:54.023 --> 00:01:00.269
The second number gives the yield strength: eighty percent of that, 640 megapascals.

16
00:01:01.013 --> 00:01:02.975
Above it the bolt stretches for good.

17
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:09.279
For ten point nine it is a thousand and 900: that one can go much tighter.

18
00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:12.964
The nut has a class too, which must match the bolt.

19
00:01:14.351 --> 00:01:17.555
A bolt fails in the thread, where there is less steel.

20
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:24.095
So engineers use the stress area: for M12, 84.3 square millimetres.

21
00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.866
Force is stress times area:

22
00:01:27.029 --> 00:01:31.591
an M12 in eight point eight yields at about 54 kilonewtons,

23
00:01:31.766 --> 00:01:34.541
in ten point nine only at 76.

24
00:01:35.243 --> 00:01:40.328
A mixed-up eight point eight reaches just 71 percent of that 76.

25
00:01:41.389 --> 00:01:44.163
Against working loose, preload helps most,

26
00:01:44.361 --> 00:01:47.496
then thread locking: an adhesive that cures in the thread.

27
00:01:48.237 --> 00:01:51.384
In vibration tests, a spring washer does not help.

28
00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:57.513
In the factory a nutrunner tightens the bolt: it controls torque and angle,

29
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.475
and records both for every bolt.

30
00:02:00.545 --> 00:02:02.217
Torque is force times arm.

31
00:02:02.927 --> 00:02:08.372
200 newtons on a wrench 25 centimetres long is 50 newton-metres.

32
00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.858
About 49 newton-metres stretches an M10 in eight

33
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.353
point eight to roughly 25 kilonewtons of preload,

34
00:02:16.527 --> 00:02:18.594
the weight of 2.5 tonnes.

35
00:02:19.355 --> 00:02:22.699
Near 37 kilonewtons it starts to yield.

36
00:02:24.059 --> 00:02:25.951
But torque is an indirect measure.

37
00:02:26.716 --> 00:02:31.789
Roughly half goes into friction under the head, about 40 percent into the thread,

38
00:02:31.998 --> 00:02:34.239
and only 10 percent stretches the bolt.

39
00:02:35.332 --> 00:02:38.212
Oil, coating, dirt or rust change the friction:

40
00:02:38.363 --> 00:02:42.473
the same torque gives 25 to 30 percent more or less preload.

41
00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.887
An oiled bolt, tightened to the dry torque, can even yield.

42
00:02:48.288 --> 00:02:52.293
More accurate is torque-angle: a low torque pulls the parts together,

43
00:02:52.514 --> 00:02:54.871
then the nut turns a fixed angle further.

44
00:02:55.593 --> 00:02:57.787
That angle is a measure of the stretch.

45
00:02:58.825 --> 00:03:02.981
An audit measures the breakaway torque: where a tightened bolt turns further.

46
00:03:03.702 --> 00:03:08.985
It should be near the set torque, here 50 newton-metres, plus or minus 10 percent.

47
00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:14.269
Measured: 53, 52, 31, 54 and 52.

48
00:03:15.032 --> 00:03:16.460
That 31 is wrong.

49
00:03:17.481 --> 00:03:20.720
One bolt was run twice, the fourth done by feel.

50
00:03:21.418 --> 00:03:24.692
With position control each bolt counts just once.

51
00:03:25.411 --> 00:03:28.116
A paint mark shows whether a bolt has turned.

52
00:03:29.203 --> 00:03:34.300
And settling: rough surfaces or paint under the head flatten, and preload is lost.

53
00:03:35.704 --> 00:03:42.392
VDI 2230, the German guideline, works through a bolted joint step by step.

54
00:03:43.452 --> 00:03:48.293
It captures the scatter in the tightening factor: highest divided by lowest preload.

55
00:03:49.058 --> 00:03:53.087
On torque, with measured friction: 1.4 to 1.6.

56
00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:57.151
The coefficient of friction, mu, matters most.

57
00:03:57.994 --> 00:04:02.383
If it is only estimated, the factor becomes 1.6 to 2.0.

58
00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:07.765
So measure it on the real bolts, to ISO 16047.

59
00:04:08.798 --> 00:04:14.185
A flange needs at least 30 kilonewtons, and the bolt may take at most 50.

60
00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:19.712
Estimated, times 1.8, the highest becomes 54: too much.

61
00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:24.438
Measured, times 1.4, it is 42: that fits.

62
00:04:25.739 --> 00:04:31.347
Torque-angle reaches a factor of 1.2 to 1.4, often past the yield point.

63
00:04:32.095 --> 00:04:34.556
Such a bolt is usually not reused.

64
00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:39.305
In yield-controlled tightening the nutrunner measures torque and angle,

65
00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:41.976
and stops as soon as the curve bends over.

66
00:04:43.027 --> 00:04:46.974
Most accurate is measuring bolt elongation by ultrasound.

67
00:04:47.742 --> 00:04:50.506
Costly and slow, so for critical joints.

68
00:04:51.804 --> 00:04:56.994
The joint diagram draws bolt and plates as two springs: steep for the stiff plates,

69
00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:58.225
flatter for the bolt.

70
00:04:59.301 --> 00:05:04.735
When 10 kilonewtons pull from outside, the bolt takes only a share: the load factor.

71
00:05:05.456 --> 00:05:08.254
At 0.2 that is 2 kilonewtons.

72
00:05:09.243 --> 00:05:11.611
The other 8 take clamp load away.

73
00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:19.590
Of the lowest preload, 18.75 kilonewtons, 10.75 remain: the residual clamp load.

74
00:05:20.395 --> 00:05:24.900
It must stay above zero, or the bolt takes the full alternating force.

75
00:05:25.903 --> 00:05:30.431
Bolts almost always break by fatigue, in the first thread under the nut.

76
00:05:31.211 --> 00:05:33.231
What counts is the stress amplitude,

77
00:05:33.394 --> 00:05:39.280
half the stress swing: 1 kilonewton on 58 square millimetres, 17 megapascals.

78
00:05:39.978 --> 00:05:41.708
The thread takes about 50.

79
00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.843
A stronger class hardly helps there.

80
00:05:45.576 --> 00:05:49.338
A slim bolt with a long clamp length, the thickness it clamps,

81
00:05:49.523 --> 00:05:54.179
does: it takes less of the alternating force, and loses less to settling.
